{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Not a man, etc., and a son of man, etc.</b> He said two levels corresponding to two aspects: one, when a person makes a promise to another concerning some matter; and the second, when a person resolves to do something in his own conduct and affairs that does not touch upon another. Corresponding to the matter that is a promise to another, he said <b>not a man is El, that He should lie</b>; and corresponding to the matter that does not touch upon another, he said <b>and a son of man, that He should relent</b> — that He does not lie to another, but rather relents concerning what He had spoken to do. And in the matter before us there are two things that HaShem spoke: one, to establish the kingdom of Israel and to do good to them; and the second, that HaShem intended to do His will to give His Torah to His holy people — and these are matters that touch upon Him, blessed be He. And corresponding to both of them He said: ״לֹא אִישׁ אֵל וִיכַזֵּב״ — regarding the good promises that He promised to the fathers; ״וּבֶן אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָם״ — from what He intended in the conduct of His world to give His Torah to Israel and to guide them to fulfill His commandments. And if so, how could He intend to curse Israel and thereby be found, Heaven forbid, lying in His promises to the fathers and relenting from what He intended to do?" "<b>And His saying ״וִיכַזֵּב״ ״וְיִתְנֶחָם״</b> with the addition of the vav, He thereby included two levels: one, that He is not a man and not a human; and furthermore, to suspect in Him, Heaven forbid, something that is considered a deficiency even among created beings — namely, lying and regretting. And He intended by this that it is impossible for Him to agree to curse Israel unless two things were found in Him: ״אִישׁ וִיכַזֵּב״ and ״בֶן אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָם״ — for even if, Heaven forbid, He were a man who lies, despite all this He would not regret what touches upon what He intended to do — to fulfill His will through the medium of Israel. And the reason He once said ״אִישׁ״ and once said ״בֶּן אָדָם״ is because one who promises to do good for another holds a certain stature; therefore he used a term of importance, whereas one who regrets what he intended for himself — he called ״בֶּן אָדָם״." "<b>He has said, etc.</b> The plain sense of the verse returns to man, but it may also be expounded of the Holy One, blessed be He, in this manner: when He wishes to perform an act, He has no need for action — rather through speech it is done. And that is His saying <b>הַהוּא</b> — meaning, the El that He mentioned nearby; ״אָמַר״ — meaning, His deeds that He performed: He said and it came to be. <b>And will He not do it</b> — meaning, He has no need for action, as He said (Genesis 1:3) ״וַיֹּאמֶר וגו׳ וַיְהִי כֵן״, and likewise they said in the Mishnah (Avot 5:1) ״בַּעֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת נִבְרָא הָעוֹלָם״. And His saying <b>וְדִבֶּר</b>, etc. — meaning, when the act performed through His speech is not altogether firmly established, after the manner of their saying that the world was unstable and trembling, as it is written (Job 26:11) ״עַמּוּדֵי שָׁמַיִם יְרוֹפָפוּ וְיִתְמְהוּ מִגַּעֲרָתוֹ״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Ḥagigah 12b) that HaShem rebuked them and they were established. And that is His saying <b>וְדִבֶּר</b> — an expression of harshness; <b>וְלֹא יְקִימֶנָּה</b> — meaning, He does not need to establish it, but rather through His harsh speech it is established and strengthened."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:26.561401999Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}